Implement user story AMSD-2041: [GO, UP, MX] Live Preview of Content in CMS

## Story Description
[GO, UP, MX] Live Preview of Content in CMS

## Acceptance Criteria
- 


## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/pages/[[...slug]].tsx** (`getStaticProps`): This is the primary content-fetching entry point for all CMS pages. It currently reads exclusively from filesystem cache (readFromFs, readCachedPageDataFromFs) which contains only published content. Contentstack live preview requires this function (or an equivalent server-side path) to detect preview mode and fetch draft/unpublished content directly from the Contentstack API instead of the static cache. The same pattern applies to the station detail page's getStaticProps in src/pages/find-a-station/[stationCode]/[contentstackUrl].tsx.
  - **Minimal fix:** Add a Next.js API route (e.g., src/pages/api/preview.ts) that: (1) receives Contentstack preview tokens from the CMS authoring UI, (2) calls Next.js res.setPreviewData with the preview context, (3) redirects to the requested page slug. Then in getStaticProps, when context.preview is true, bypass readFromFs/readCachedPageDataFromFs and instead call the Contentstack content delivery API directly (using the existing getEntry/getSingletonEntry helpers already imported in the station page) with the preview token to fetch draft content. The minimal change is: guard each readFromFs call with a preview-mode check that falls through to a live API call when context.preview exists, and add the preview API route to enable entering preview mode from Contentstack.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- Given a content author has saved a draft edit to a CMS-driven page in Contentstack, when the author opens that page in preview, then the rendered page displays the draft content the author just edited, not the previously published version.
- Given a content author is viewing a CMS-driven page in preview, when the author makes and saves a further edit in Contentstack, then the author observes the page update to show the newly edited content without requiring the content to be published.
- Given a content author makes several sequential edits to the same CMS-driven page while previewing, when each edit is saved, then the author observes each successive version of the content appear on the page in turn.
- Given a visitor browses a CMS-driven page on the live site, when the page loads, then the visitor sees the published content rendered normally, with no preview or draft content visible.
- Given a content author opens a page in preview while the draft content cannot be retrieved, when the page loads, then the author observes the page render in a usable state (showing available content or a clear fallback) rather than a broken or blank page.

## Tests are NOT your job this turn
A dedicated test-writer agent runs immediately after your fix commits and owns the bug-reproducing test. Do NOT write, edit, or create any test file (*.test.*, *.spec.*, __tests__/). Write ONLY the fix. Adding a test here wastes your turn budget and has caused repeated failures.

